@meta
id: 02-01
title: Saving is not leaving
chapter: 02
type: lesson
skills: write
par: 45
minutes: 3
vscode: Ctrl+S
open: release-notes.md

@brief
In VS Code, Ctrl+S is so cheap that most people hit it every few seconds
without thinking. The vim equivalent is `:w`, and it is just as cheap.
What it is not is a way out of the file: `:w` writes the buffer to disk
and leaves you exactly where you were, cursor and all.

`:wq` is two commands stuck together, and chapter 1 handed it to you a
little too early. Reaching for it every time you want to save means
quitting and reopening a file you are still working in.

Two release notes are unfinished.

    - The refund endpoint now returns 202 Accepted
    - Customer search ignores case and leading whitespace

Complete the first line, save. Complete the second line, save. Then quit.
Two writes, one quit, and you never leave the file in between.

@keys
| `:w` | write the buffer to disk, stay in the file |
| `:q` | quit — refuses if there are unsaved changes |
| `:wq` | write, then quit |

@hints
The lines you need are three and four rows below the cursor. `j` moves
down one row.
--
Both fixes are words missing from the *end* of a line. `$` goes to the
last character, and `a` starts typing after it.
--
After `Esc`, type `:w` and press Enter. The file is saved and the cursor
has not moved. Do the second line, `:w` again, then `:q`.

@solution
    jjj $ a Accepted <Esc> :w<Enter>
    j $ a whitespace <Esc> :w<Enter>
    :q<Enter>

The habit worth building is writing at every natural pause, not at the
end. Vim will not lose your work to a crash if it is already on disk, and
`:w` costs three keystrokes.

`:q` on its own is the honest quit. If the buffer has unsaved changes it
refuses with `E37: No write since last change`, which is vim telling you
it would rather annoy you than throw work away. Two ways past that
message: save it, or say you meant it. The next two lessons are those two
ways.

@start release-notes.md
# Release 2.14

- Orders placed before the cutoff are no longer charged twice
- The refund endpoint now returns 202
- Customer search ignores case and leading

@expected release-notes.md
# Release 2.14

- Orders placed before the cutoff are no longer charged twice
- The refund endpoint now returns 202 Accepted
- Customer search ignores case and leading whitespace
